  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Partial fit. The specimen is strong on keeping byte carriage separate from payload semantics, but it does not define the UDP-feed binding or explicit behavior for non-DAG-CBOR malformed datagrams, so the scenario pressure is only partly answered.",
    "strengths": [
      "Strong layer boundary: `payload` stays opaque until the `pcid` handler, and speculative parsing is forbidden.",
      "Unknown-`pcid` handling is explicit and can preserve exact envelope bytes for store/forward.",
      "The design supports evolution and sparse knowledge without assuming a central pCID registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No UDP-feed binding is specified, so byte-transparent transport conformance is indirect rather than explicit.",
      "The exact-byte preservation rule is clear for unknown `pcid`, but not for bytes that never parse as an envelope at all.",
      "Mandatory signature slots may impose envelope semantics too early if a binding treats every datagram as an envelope."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementations may diverge on whether malformed datagrams are dropped, surfaced raw, or only logged.",
      "Underspecified outer parse-failure behavior could weaken later audit of corrupted or adversarial traffic."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What local record should Bob keep when a datagram fails outer DAG-CBOR parsing?",
      "Where is the UDP-feed-to-envelope binding specified for this specimen, if anywhere?",
      "Should raw malformed datagrams be passed upward unchanged, stored as opaque artifacts, or treated as terminal errors?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
